        <rect x="106" y="46.5" width="4" height="43" class="scr"/>
        <circle cx="66" cy="60" r="9" class="knob"/><line x1="66" y1="60" x2="66" y2="53" class="tick"/>
        <circle cx="90" cy="80" r="6" class="knob"/><line x1="90" y1="80" x2="94" y2="76" class="tick"/>
        <polygon points="48,96 80,96 86,186 56,186" class="shell"/>
        <rect x="40" y="102" width="10" height="19" rx="4" class="recf"/>
        <rect x="28.5" y="53" width="3" height="32" class="part"/>
        <rect x="38" y="88" width="50" height="4" class="part"/>
        <rect x="59" y="108" width="18" height="65" rx="8" class="part" transform="rotate(-3.8 68 140)"/>
        <rect x="64" y="183" width="9" height="3" class="glass"/>

        <line x1="12.5" y1="200" x2="110" y2="200" class="dim"/><line x1="12.5" y1="196" x2="12.5" y2="204" class="dim"/><line x1="110" y1="196" x2="110" y2="204" class="dim"/>
        <text x="40" y="210" class="lbl-ink">≈ 98 long</text>
        <line x1="120" y1="35.5" x2="120" y2="186" class="dim"/><line x1="116" y1="35.5" x2="124" y2="35.5" class="dim"/><line x1="116" y1="186" x2="124" y2="186" class="dim"/>
        <text x="123" y="115" class="lbl-ink">≈ 150</text>

        <text x="226" y="12" class="view">Rear</text>
        <rect x="236" y="32" width="100" height="80" rx="10" class="part"/>
        <rect x="251" y="40" width="70" height="56" rx="8" class="shell"/>
        <rect x="256" y="46.5" width="60" height="43" rx="2" class="scr"/>
        <circle cx="313" cy="92.5" r="2" class="recf"/>
        <rect x="271" y="96" width="30" height="90" rx="7" class="shell"/>
        <line x1="251" y1="200" x2="321" y2="200" class="dim"/><line x1="251" y1="196" x2="251" y2="204" class="dim"/><line x1="321" y1="196" x2="321" y2="204" class="dim"/>
        <text x="272" y="210" class="lbl-ink">70 wide</text>
        <text x="256" y="226" class="lbl">2.4" 320×240 screen</text>
      </svg>
    </div>
    <div class="cv-table">
      <table>
        <thead><tr><th></th><th>Original A</th><th>A-mini</th></tr></thead>
        <tbody>
          <tr><th>Screen</th><td>3.5" 480×320</td><td>2.4" 320×240, exactly 2× the 160×120 image</td></tr>
          <tr><th>Body (L × H × W)</th><td class="cv-num">150 × 80 × 100</td><td class="cv-num">80 × 56 × 70</td></tr>
          <tr><th>With lens and grip</th><td class="cv-num">≈ 180 × 190</td><td class="cv-num">≈ 98 × 150</td></tr>
          <tr><th>Shell plastic, 2 mm walls</th><td class="cv-num">≈ 170 cm³</td><td class="cv-num">≈ 75 cm³</td></tr>
        </tbody>
      </table>
    </div>
    <div class="cv-facts">
      <div><h4>What makes it smaller</h4><p>The 2.4" screen sets a 70 × 56 rear face, about the size of a 1990s camcorder's screen. The P4-NANO lies flat in the bottom and the camera stands at the front, so the length is set by the board plus clearance.</p></div>
      <div><h4>Grip</h4><p>90 mm tall, a three-to-four-finger grip shaped around one 18650 cell, with the trigger REC at the top front. Printed as its own part, so the body's bounding box stays small.</p></div>
      <div><h4>REC</h4><p>A printed trigger lever pressing one of the 12 mm tactile switches on hand. The 30 mm arcade buttons are too deep for a body this size; use them on the bench.</p></div>
      <div><h4>Shrinks again with the PCB</h4><p>A custom board shaped like the rear face, with the screen mounted on it, removes the dev board's wasted space and headers. It could take another 15–20 mm off the length.</p></div>
    </div>
    <ul class="cv-notes">
      <li><strong>Plastic volume roughly halves</strong>, and service print prices mostly follow plastic volume and part size. Exact prices depend on the service and material.</li>
      <li>Print it as three small parts: front shell, rear bezel and grip. Smaller parts are cheaper, orient better and are easier to reprint one at a time.</li>
      <li>Do fit checks in cheap PLA (your own printer, a makerspace or a library) and order the expensive finish once the shape is right.</li>
      <li>This trades away some of the handoff's "chunky toy-camera" bulk. It's still a pistol-grip camera you point and watch, just pocket-sized.</li>
      <li>The 2.4" screen is a new part. The 1.54" panel arriving now is fine for bench work in the meantime.</li>
    </ul>
  </section>

  <section class="cv-sheet" aria-labelledby="cv-parts-h">
