        <rect x="116" y="11" width="85" height="56" rx="2" class="scr"/>
        <text x="116" y="74" class="lbl-ink">3.5" screen</text><text x="116" y="82" class="lbl">≈ 85 × 56</text>

        <rect x="214" y="6" width="18" height="65" rx="8" class="partsolid"/>
        <text x="210" y="80" class="lbl-ink">18650</text><text x="210" y="88" class="lbl">18 × 65</text>

        <rect x="246" y="22" width="50" height="34" rx="3" class="partsolid"/>
        <text x="246" y="74" class="lbl-ink">LiPo 2000</text><text x="246" y="82" class="lbl">≈ 50 × 34 × 10</text>

        <circle cx="325" cy="39" r="15" class="recf"/>
        <text x="310" y="74" class="lbl-ink">Arcade REC</text><text x="310" y="82" class="lbl">30 ø, ≈ 35 deep</text>

        <line x1="6" y1="95" x2="56" y2="95" class="dim"/><line x1="6" y1="92" x2="6" y2="98" class="dim"/><line x1="56" y1="92" x2="56" y2="98" class="dim"/>
        <text x="62" y="97" class="lbl">50 mm</text>
      </svg>
    </div>
    <ul class="cv-notes">
      <li><strong>The screen sets the face it sits on.</strong> A 3.5" panel needs a face of about 95 × 66 mm. The original PXL-2000 body was only about 57 mm wide, so a rear screen makes this camera wider than the original.</li>
      <li><strong>The grip is the battery bay.</strong> Super 8 cameras like the Canon 514XL kept their batteries in the handle. An 18650 cell (18 × 65 mm) fits a grip almost exactly and is sturdier than a pouch.</li>
      <li><strong>Arcade buttons are deep.</strong> With the microswitch, the 30 mm arcade REC buttons on hand need about 35 mm behind the panel. That suits the top of the body, not a thin grip.</li>
      <li>The P4-NANO and camera are small. Space goes to the screen, battery, grip and controls, which is what the handoff asked for.</li>
    </ul>
  </section>

