  <section class="cv-sheet cv-pick" aria-labelledby="cv-pick-h">
    <h3 id="cv-pick-h">Where I'd started (before the decision)</h3>
    <p><strong>A, with C as the wildcard.</strong> A is the clearest answer to "hand it to someone and they shoot without instructions," and it's the easiest to print and wire. C has the most personality, and it's the one I'd mock up next to A to see which feels better in the hand.</p>
    <p>Decide the screen size before anything else. A 2.8" panel would shrink A to about 80 mm wide, and the 1.54" bench screen makes everything toy-sized. Cardboard settles it faster than drawings.</p>
  </section>

  <section class="cv-sheet" aria-labelledby="cv-next-h">
    <div class="cv-sheet-head"><h3 id="cv-next-h">Next: a cardboard afternoon</h3></div>
    <ul class="cv-notes">
      <li>Build A-mini as a cardboard box at 80 × 56 × 70 mm with a 90 mm grip. Tape a phone showing a 160×120 clip where the screen goes (masked to 49 × 37 mm, the 2.4" active area), and put a 50 g weight in the grip for the 18650.</li>
      <li>Walk around outside with it. Check that a 2.4" screen reads at arm's length in sun, where your thumb and index finger land, and that the body isn't too small to hold steady.</li>
      <li>Try the grip angle: upright like the PXL-2000's handle, or raked back like a Super 8.</li>
      <li>Then I'll turn the winner into a dimensioned layout: the PCB outline, mounting holes, and where USB-C, the SD card and the switch come out.</li>
    </ul>
  </section>

